⚡ SwiftUI Concurrency Patterns (Day 5)

SwiftUI deeply integrates with Swift Concurrency (async/await).
Most modern apps rely on:

.task
Task
@MainActor
cancellation
structured concurrency

This is a high-priority interview topic.

🧠 Core Principle
Async work should follow the lifecycle of the View

SwiftUI gives you tools to tie async work to UI lifecycle automatically.

1️⃣ .task — The Correct Way
Example
struct ContentView: View {

    var body: some View {
        Text("Loading...")
            .task {
                await fetchData()
            }
    }
}
Why .task is powerful
- Runs when view appears
- Cancels automatically when view disappears
- Supports async/await
2️⃣ .onAppear vs .task
❌ .onAppear
.onAppear {
    loadData() // not cancellable
}
✅ .task
.task {
    await loadData()
}
Key Difference
.task → lifecycle-aware + cancellable  
.onAppear → fire-and-forget
3️⃣ @MainActor — UI Safety

UI updates must happen on the main thread.

@MainActor
final class ViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var data: String = ""
}
Why this matters

Prevents:

Publishing changes from background thread crash
4️⃣ Cancellation (Very Important)

SwiftUI cancels tasks automatically.

.task {
    await fetchData()
}

If the view disappears:

Task is cancelled automatically
Manual cancellation check
func fetchData() async {
    guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }

    // continue work
}
5️⃣ Using Task {} — When Needed
Example
Button("Load") {
    Task {
        await fetchData()
    }
}
When to use
user-triggered actions
button taps
retry logic
⚠️ Danger
Task {} is NOT lifecycle-bound
6️⃣ Structured Concurrency
Parallel execution
async let a = fetchA()
async let b = fetchB()

let result = await (a, b)
Benefits
faster execution
clean syntax
no callback hell
7️⃣ Common Concurrency Bugs
❌ Bug 1 — Updating UI off main thread
DispatchQueue.global().async {
    self.label = "Hello" // crash
}
❌ Bug 2 — Using unowned self in async
Task {
    [unowned self] in // ❌ crash risk
}
❌ Bug 3 — Ignoring cancellation
await fetchData() // continues even if view gone
❌ Bug 4 — Multiple overlapping tasks
Multiple API calls triggered unintentionally
8️⃣ Best Practice Pattern
.task {
    await viewModel.load()
}

ViewModel:

@MainActor
func load() async {
    state = .loading

    do {
        let data = try await service.fetch()
        state = .loaded(data)
    } catch {
        state = .error
    }
}
9️⃣ Debouncing / Task Cancellation Pattern
.task(id: searchText) {
    try? await Task.sleep(nanoseconds: 300_000_000)

    guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }

    await search()
}

Used for:

search bars
live filtering
🔥 Advanced Insight

SwiftUI uses:

Structured concurrency + automatic cancellation

This is why .task is preferred.

🧠 Interview Power Answer

If asked:

“How do you handle async work in SwiftUI?”

Answer:

“I use .task for lifecycle-bound async work, Task {} for user-triggered actions, ensure ViewModels are @MainActor, and handle cancellation properly.”

⭐ Golden Rules
Prefer .task over .onAppear
Use @MainActor for UI updates
Handle cancellation
Avoid unowned in async
Use structured concurrency
